WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.826
Our shop's checkout now asks two services at once.

2
00:00:05.826 --> 00:00:12.814
The stock service says whether the items are in the warehouse, and the bank says whether the payment is approved.

3
00:00:12.814 --> 00:00:14.793
That's a fan-out, from lesson 3.

4
00:00:14.793 --> 00:00:19.123
Each call is a subtask: a piece of the job, on its own virtual thread.

5
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.113
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

6
00:00:22.113 --> 00:00:25.595
The checkout waits for every subtask before it returns.

7
00:00:25.595 --> 00:00:27.241
Isn't that careful enough?

8
00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:31.022
Let's test it.

9
00:00:31.022 --> 00:00:36.070
The items are sold out, so the stock service fails after 100 milliseconds.

10
00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:39.481
The bank is having a bad day, and takes 2 seconds.

11
00:00:39.481 --> 00:00:41.255
Its answer is useless now.

12
00:00:41.255 --> 00:00:43.369
So when does the checkout fail?

13
00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:53.519
The lesson's LeakDemo ran it, with an executor, an object that runs tasks for you, made for each checkout.

14
00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:58.214
It failed at 2,069 milliseconds: doomed at 100, failed at 2 seconds.

15
00:00:58.214 --> 00:01:03.530
Leaving the try block waits for every task, and nobody told the bank to stop.

16
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:09.053
So it took its full two seconds to answer a question nobody was asking any more.

17
00:01:09.133 --> 00:01:12.126
Many programs share one executor instead.

18
00:01:12.126 --> 00:01:21.030
Then the checkout fails fast, at 102 milliseconds, but the bank call runs on until 2,001, for a checkout that's long gone.

19
00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:25.409
Under load these orphans pile up, each holding a connection.

20
00:01:26.133 --> 00:01:27.306
Now the restaurant.

21
00:01:27.306 --> 00:01:36.938
A big table orders, and the head waiter sends three helpers at once: to the kitchen for the soup, to the bar for the drinks, and to the cellar for the wine.

22
00:01:36.938 --> 00:01:40.148
Then he stays at the table until all three are back.

23
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.426
Now the cellar is out of that wine.

24
00:01:42.426 --> 00:01:46.374
Its helper comes straight back, and the table changes its order.

25
00:01:46.374 --> 00:01:49.766
A careful head waiter calls the other two back at once.

26
00:01:49.766 --> 00:01:55.256
A careless one lets them carry on, and a soup arrives for an order that no longer exists.

27
00:01:55.256 --> 00:01:56.490
Someone will eat it.

28
00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.971
Probably the dishwasher.

29
00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:01.846
So the point is: waiting for everyone is only half the job.

30
00:02:01.846 --> 00:02:04.216
When one fails, the others must stop.

31
00:02:04.216 --> 00:02:06.394
Our executor was the careless one.

32
00:02:06.394 --> 00:02:09.084
Version A waited for a soup nobody wanted.

33
00:02:09.084 --> 00:02:12.159
Version B left while the soup was still cooking.

34
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.768
So let's hire the careful one, and map him part by part.

35
00:02:16.768 --> 00:02:21.546
The head waiter is the scope, an object that starts subtasks and owns them.

36
00:02:21.546 --> 00:02:25.369
Sending a helper is fork, a subtask on a new virtual thread.

37
00:02:25.369 --> 00:02:27.217
Waiting at the table is join.

38
00:02:27.217 --> 00:02:29.574
Calling the others back is fail fast.

39
00:02:29.574 --> 00:02:32.632
And staying until every helper is back is close.

40
00:02:33.333 --> 00:02:35.403
Same checkout, now in a scope.

41
00:02:35.403 --> 00:02:40.716
The stock check fails at 100 milliseconds, and the bank would take 2 seconds.

42
00:02:40.716 --> 00:02:42.441
When does join throw now?

43
00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:47.061
At 0, the scope forks both.

44
00:02:47.061 --> 00:02:50.118
At 100 milliseconds, the stock subtask throws.

45
00:02:50.118 --> 00:02:54.372
The scope keeps that as the first failure, and cancels the rest.

46
00:02:54.372 --> 00:03:00.353
Cancel means interrupt: a signal that wakes a waiting thread with an InterruptedException.

47
00:03:00.353 --> 00:03:04.607
So the bank subtask, waiting on the network, wakes up and stops.

48
00:03:04.733 --> 00:03:08.372
So join throws the stock failure at about 100 milliseconds.

49
00:03:08.372 --> 00:03:12.011
Then close waits until the bank thread has really finished.

50
00:03:12.011 --> 00:03:15.527
When the block ends, nothing it started is still running.

51
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.849
That has a name: structured concurrency.

52
00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:26.069
A subtask can't outlive the block of code that started it, just as a local variable can't outlive its method.

53
00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:28.357
Here the picture breaks.

54
00:03:28.357 --> 00:03:30.693
A real helper hears his name called.

55
00:03:30.693 --> 00:03:36.988
A subtask hears the interrupt only while it waits: in a sleep, a network read, a lock or a queue.

56
00:03:36.988 --> 00:03:41.335
A loop that only computes hears nothing, like a helper in earmuffs.

57
00:03:41.335 --> 00:03:44.775
Cancelling is a request, and the code must cooperate.

58
00:03:45.467 --> 00:03:49.621
The JDK has its own head waiter, StructuredTaskScope.

59
00:03:49.621 --> 00:03:59.106
In JDK 25 it's a preview: finished, but allowed to change in the next release, so the compiler refuses it without a flag.

60
00:03:59.106 --> 00:04:01.849
It's due to become final in JDK 28.

61
00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:06.706
The lesson's ScopeDemo runs our checkout that way on JDK 25.

62
00:04:06.706 --> 00:04:15.058
At 135 milliseconds the bank was interrupted, and at 165 the checkout failed, with nothing still running.

63
00:04:15.058 --> 00:04:19.831
A little over 100, because the first run also loads classes.

64
00:04:20.733 --> 00:04:25.283
One gap is left: the order's own details, like its number for the log.

65
00:04:25.283 --> 00:04:32.821
A ThreadLocal, the apron pocket from lesson 4, doesn't follow the work into the helpers, and anyone can write in it.

66
00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:35.259
So pin a note to the order instead.

67
00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:41.040
Every helper can read it, nobody can change it, and it comes off when the order closes.

68
00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:47.684
That's a scoped value: bound for one call with where and run, read with get, and with no set at all.

69
00:04:47.684 --> 00:04:50.475
It's final in JDK 25, so it needs no flag.

70
00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.919
It flows into the subtasks a scope forks.

71
00:04:53.919 --> 00:04:57.035
That's the order-42 on every line of ScopeDemo.

72
00:04:57.035 --> 00:04:59.753
A thread you start yourself gets nothing.

73
00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:04.968
Here's the checkout in JDK 25, Java on top and Kotlin below.

74
00:05:04.968 --> 00:05:09.845
Fork both, join, and let close make sure nothing is left running.

75
00:05:10.533 --> 00:05:13.569
So, is waiting for every helper careful enough?

76
00:05:13.569 --> 00:05:13.763
No.

77
00:05:13.763 --> 00:05:18.478
A careful head waiter also calls the others back, and never leaves early.

78
00:05:18.478 --> 00:05:21.643
A scope does both: no subtask outlives its block.

79
00:05:21.643 --> 00:05:25.841
And the note on the order goes wherever the scope sends the work.

80
00:05:25.933 --> 00:05:26.718
Quick check.

81
00:05:26.718 --> 00:05:29.464
The stock check fails at 100 milliseconds.

82
00:05:29.464 --> 00:05:31.752
The bank call waits on the network.

83
00:05:31.752 --> 00:05:36.459
A third subtask runs a 3 second loop that never checks for an interrupt.

84
00:05:36.459 --> 00:05:39.663
When does join throw, and when does close return?

85
00:05:42.867 --> 00:05:46.992
Join throws at about 100 milliseconds, with the stock failure.

86
00:05:46.992 --> 00:05:49.188
Close returns at about 3 seconds.

87
00:05:49.188 --> 00:05:55.111
The interrupt stops the network wait, but not the loop, and close waits for every thread.
